	I looked up at the sky.  Against the space-black sky were stars. More stars,
brighter stars, more colorful stars than I had ever seen.  I had just earlier that day
landed on Sardinica and this was my first assignment as an astrophysical observer.  My job
was to observe the Galactic Center's black hole.  I laid down on the imported
earth grass and began to connect the dots in the sky.  I imagined whales, space stations,
Fynish cloud creatures and then started thinking what a field day ancient astrologers
would have had with a sky like this when I heard a young Mexu ask, "maybe, you wanta buy
a Yescim?"
	I had heard of Yescims before, but never had I seen one.  He was a handsome animal.
Soft features, slow curves and a beautiful shiny green fur.  The Mexu kid went on,
"Pleez lady.  I sell it cheap.  In good health.  Pleez lady."
	It offended my sense of morality, but the kid pleaded, he was beautiful,
I was lonely on a faraway planet with no friends, and he offered to sell the Yescim
for only 100 credits.  I easily haggled the price down to 38 credits ---- only half a
day's wages.
	He was untrained but cooperative.  He quickly learned my language.  He was
my pet, my slave, my servant, my companion, and was even good for some old-fashioned 
sex.  Always helpful, always cooperative, always beautiful ---- he was great.  He
was a good learner; I soon had him helping me with the more routine astronomical
observations.
	As he learned more, my respect for him grew.  He could participate in a
technical conversation about complex inner-stellar nuclear reactions or bake a bubble
steak to perfection.  I began to think of him as my intellectual equal and began to
question my right to own him.  Eventually I concluded that I should teach him to be
independent and then set him free.
	I asked him, "Don't you ever want to be on your own, to disobey, to be free."
 I knew him well enough not to be surprised by his answer, "No."
	I continued, "Do you know what it's like to be free?"
	"Sure. It's a great joy and challenge to do as one pleases, to be your own
boss."
	"How do you know all that?"
	"All I know I have been told by someone or have read what someone had written."
	I fully anticipated that answer, but I had a counter argument
I wanted to try.
	"But you have eyes, noses and a rade.  You can feel, peep and taste.  Don't
you know what your senses tell you corresponds to reality?"
	"If you say so, then it's true.  All knowledge is secondary ---- a Yescim knows
only what it has been told.  I heard it expressed well once, 'All knowledge is second
hand.'"
	I thought I had him then.  "But how do you know that, 'All knowledge is
second hand'?"
	"I don't know how I know it, only that I do.  This same Yescim called it the
'Great Paradox of Life' and said that Yescims just know it and that is that."
	I retreated from this line of attack, and asked him if there was anything
that he desired if freedom did not appeal to him.  He made the first request I ever
heard from him, "There is one thing, that if it pleases you, I would like to 
accomplish.  That is I would like to procreate and be with a female
Yescim for an afternoon.  That is all I ask, except that if it upsets you, you should
refuse my request."
	I don't know whether my Yescim was a genius in human psychology or just happened
to ask the question in such a way that, despite my jealousies, I had to allow him this
simple request.  That afternoon I felt terrible and realized for the first time that I
loved my Yescim.
	Six months later I was surprised by the delivery of a small red Yescim only
a few weeks old.  I asked the deliverer why I received such a gift.  It was explained
to me that female Yescims always give birth to a red female Yescim and a green male.
The female child is given of the household to the father and the male is kept by the
female's owners.
I then asked what it was that I should do with the baby.  The answer shocked me, "Well,
you can sell her to the Yescim factory for fur and meat or else if you bring it up you
can sell it for more than the food costs since Yescims can eat almost anything.  But most
people sell them to the factories since they pay better prices."
	After accepting the baby, I immediately went to my Yescim for information.  I
realized that I had been too involved in my work here on Sardinica and had not learned
enough about the local customs, especially those involving Yescims.  
	I asked him why it was that Yescims are treated so badly, either as a slave or
as food and clothing.  He didn't know either but promised to study the matter and tell
me what he learned.
	I went to my computer console and asked for information concerning Yescims.
Quickly a summary formed on my screen.
.begin narrow 6,6 single space
	Yescim is the name of a genus of unknown planetary origin.  They are especially
prevalent in the star-dense galactic center.  They are perhaps the most common form
of life in the entire Galaxy.  On planets of the Human Federation they are used for
food, fur, a milk-like substance they produce and on the more backward outposts as work
animals.  They have very sophisticated digestive systems and as such can eat most
green plants, including grass and leaves, fruits, vegetables, and meats.
Many have been trained to perform many mundane, unpleasant or dangerous
tasks.  They are very subservient and quick learners.  Those Yescims on planets
dominated by non-human races are very different in form, yet are typically useful
for most of the aliens' needs.
.end
	Even after reading that and the details that followed, that which was so
obvious later had not crossed my mind.  My thoughts were interrupted by the lights
flashing on my automatic observation devices.  I went to see what anomaly they had
detected and saw to my horror a fleet of gigantic ships were headed towards this
sector of space.  No one knew what types of creatures inhabited these huge ships
only that occasionally reports are sent out describing the appearance of such ships
in an area and no further word ever comes.  Ships sent to explore the region never
come back.
	I sent off the appropriate messages to the commander of Sardinica and to various
offices in the space network.  But I had little hope, I assumed I had only a few hours
to live.  I went to my Yescim and made love with a furiousity caused by the imminent
end.  After a few hours I noticed a strange odor and then an eery blue
light.  What seemed like the 
next moment I found myself in a room with many other humans, all of whom were
apparently prisoners, just as I was, but I was glad for at least I was still alive.
A voice appeared without any apparent source and said that we were now to do as told
if we wanted to live.  Each of us were given assignments and went to work fearing for
our lives.  
	I was fortunate that my job involved operating some transportation vehicles
for I had many questions, among them what happened to the Yescims ---- had they been
behind this attack, was it that they had turned the tables?  I thought of my Yescim
and found that I could not believe this possibility.  I wondered if they 
were they destroyed or 
were also slaves of the new Overlords.  The next day I found the answer to my
question ---- they were slaves just as I was.
	Months passed and it was like hell.  All the humans hated the new Overlords,
they wanted freedom once again.  They slowly, very secretly, organized a rebellion plan.
The plan was based on the fact that the humans controlled essentially all the 
communication and transportation devices on the planet.  The huge ships had left,
presumably to expand their empire.  It was suspected that few Overlords had stayed behind.
	The night before the rebellion was to take place, I began to think that it
might be my last night.  The plan was dangerous, but I knew that life without freedom
was not worth living.  Thoughts of imminent death reminded me of the day of the
Takeover.  I remembered the hours I spent with my Yescim and longed to see him just one
more time.
	It was difficult, the details of which are not worth recording, but I finally
located him late that night.  He was as beautiful as ever and I was happy for the
first time in months.  But my joy was short lived for I quickly discovered that he
had changed.  He no longer was interested in me, he had new masters now.  I saw
that he was as friendly, cooperative towards his new maters as he had been to me.
	I ran away crying, feeling worse than I had ever believed possible.  I don't
remember very well what happened during 
the rest of the night, but I woke up with the sun
directly overhead.  The rebellion was to have taken place at dawn.  I wandered 
towards the center of town, no longer caring much what the outcome was.  It turned
out the we lost, the Overlords were smarter than us, though not smart enough to
prevent the loss of 99 percent of the human population of Sardinica.  The Overlords were
not too concerned though; the Yescims were reproducing.
